  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA cold front and associated weak low pressure will move east of\nthe forecast area early this morning. The showers are likely to\nmove offshore well before sunrise. Thereafter, clearing quickly\ntake place along with the quick dissipation of any fog. The\nbalance of the day will feature warm temperatures, mostly sunny\nskies, and wind gusts of 20 to 25 MPH from the NW.\n\nSFC high pressure will impinge on the forecast area from the NW\ntonight which will allow the winds to become light. Portions of\nthe northern mountains (valleys) may decouple enough to allow \nfor radiational cooling that delivers lows in the 30s allowing \npossible areas of frost. 40s elsewhere.\n\nThe SFC high crests over the forecast area on Saturday allowing\nfor a lot of sun, lighter winds, and cooler high temperatures.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe highest confidence portion of the extended is that the\npattern will generally favor high pressure locally thru next\nweek. More than that high pressure is looking seasonably strong.\nAbsent any cloud cover that will mean relatively shallow mixing\nduring the day, and overnight ideal conditions for radiational\ncooling. The latest run of the NBM has daytime temps cooler than\nnormal, staying in the 60s, while overnight plenty of 40s and\nmid to upper 30s in the usual colder valleys. I suspect frost\nwill be possible, but that will be dependent on any cloud cover\non individual nights. I do not plan to put any in the forecast\nat this range, but I want to mention it here as a threat.\n\nThe cloud cover will be tied to the surface baroclinic zone. The\nhigh pressure should tend to keep that south of the forecast\narea, but that does not mean that any wave tracking along it\ncan't spread clouds and even rain/showers thru parts of the\narea. Given the strong ridge centered across the southeastern\nCONUS, the pattern is fairly favorable for a later season ring\nof fire. So the convective cycle could drive these waves, and\nwill lead to lower than normal model confidence. Right now\nensemble guidance likes one of these waves to pass near the\nforecast area Sun. The latest ECMWF Ens has roughly a third of\nthe members dry for the northeastern half of the forecast area,\nand the GEFS has near a 50/50 split. The latest NBM run has an\narea of likely PoP sweeping along and south of a LEB to PWM\nline, so that is a fairly good match and I see no reason to\nalter it. Ensembles are also lighter on QPF than the ongoing\nevent. We will welcome the rain but it does not look like a\ndrought buster.",
